Description: disable Rust LTO on amd64 to keep gkrust rustc under OOM threshold
Author: Harkirat S Virk <harkiratsvirk@gmail.com>
Origin: local
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-05-15
Bug-Athena: Surfaced during GROUPS-01 phase 3 gnome build 2026-05-15
 NOT a packaging bug — addresses a HOST-RESOURCE limit that surfaces
 as an opaque SIGKILL during the firefox-esr build:
 .
   error: could not compile `gkrust` (lib)
   Caused by:
     process didn't exit successfully: `... rustc ... -Clto ...` (signal: 9, SIGKILL: kill)
 .
 `gkrust` is Mozilla's monolithic Rust library for libxul.  Mozilla's
 `config/makefiles/rust.mk` appends `-Clto` to the `cargo rustc`
 invocation for the main gkrust crate UNCONDITIONALLY (gated only on
 sanitizer / coverage / gtest, NOT on MOZ_LTO).  So a mozconfig
 `--disable-lto` does NOT disable Rust LTO — it only flips
 MOZ_LTO_RUST_CROSS between thin/fat for the cross-language link.
 The cargo rustc -Clto for gkrust persists.
 .
 Under this `-Clto`, the gkrust rustc link peaks at ~6-8 GB resident.
 With our [Source] parallelism running multiple rustc workers
 concurrently, total RAM saturates and the kernel OAM-killer fires.
 cargo reports the SIGKILL as opaque `signal: 9` — the actual OOM
 message lands in host dmesg, not in the build log.
 .
 Debian itself already encountered this on memory-constrained archs
 and shipped `debian-hacks/Allow-to-override-rust-LTO-flag.patch`
 (position 9 in `debian/patches/series` — applied automatically at
 dpkg-source extraction).  That patch makes the cargo_rustc_flags
 line honour a `DEBIAN_RUST_LTO` env var:
 .
   cargo_rustc_flags += $(or $(DEBIAN_RUST_LTO),-Clto…)
 .
 …and gates the `-Cembed-bitcode=yes` line on `DEBIAN_RUST_LTO` not
 being `-Clto=off` (embed-bitcode is only required when LTO is on).
 .
 Debian's `debian/rules` then sets `DEBIAN_RUST_LTO=-Clto=thin` for
 armhf and `DEBIAN_RUST_LTO=-Clto=off` for s390x — both
 memory-constrained.  amd64 is NOT in the list, so it gets the
 default `-Clto` and OOMs on hosts with < ~24 GB headroom per
 parallel rustc.
 .
 Add an amd64 clause that mirrors the s390x branch
 (`DEBIAN_RUST_LTO=-Clto=off`).  This:
   - Skips the cargo `-Clto` for the gkrust crate (per the
     Allow-to-override-rust-LTO-flag patch's `$(or ...)` fallback)
   - Skips `-Cembed-bitcode=yes` (per the same patch's
     `ifneq (-Clto=off,$(DEBIAN_RUST_LTO))` gate)
 .
 Each gkrust rustc now peaks at ~2-3 GB instead of ~6-8 GB.
 Trade-off: ~3-5% firefox runtime perf loss from no link-time
 optimisation across the Rust codebase.  Acceptable for an
 installable-via-tasksel browser where build reliability matters
 more than peak benchmark numbers — same trade Debian already made
 on s390x.
 .
 Why not use the mozconfig `--disable-lto`: that controls MOZ_LTO
 (C/C++ LTO via lld), not the Rust LTO.  C++ LTO uses lld with
 lower per-process memory and isn't the dominant OOM contributor
 here.  The previous mozconfig-based attempt (commit 97276da)
 was at the wrong layer and had no effect on the gkrust rustc
 invocation — confirmed by the cargo rustc command line in the
 failed build log still showing `-Clto`.
 .
 Revert this patch only when paired with confirmation that the
 build host has ≥ ~24 GB RAM headroom for parallel LTO rustc on
 amd64, OR when [Source] parallelism is reduced to 1 (which would
 also cap the build at ~30-40 minutes for gkrust alone — slower
 than the ~7 min `-Clto=off` build).
---
This patch header follows DEP-3: http://dep.debian.net/deps/dep3/
--- a/debian/rules
+++ b/debian/rules
@@ -245,6 +245,12 @@
 ifeq (s390x,$(DEB_BUILD_ARCH))
 export DEBIAN_RUST_LTO=-Clto=off
 endif
+# amd64: gkrust LTO peaks at ~6-8 GB resident per rustc; with our
+# [Source] parallelism, total RAM saturates and the kernel OOM-killer
+# fires.  Cap memory via -Clto=off; same trade as s390x above.
+ifeq (amd64,$(DEB_BUILD_ARCH))
+export DEBIAN_RUST_LTO=-Clto=off
+endif

 stamps/build-$(PRODUCT):: stamps/configure-check-$(PRODUCT)
 	+dh_auto_build --parallel --builddirectory=build-$(PRODUCT)
